WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:05.520
It's an edgy cartoon about crime-fighting&nbsp;
cryptids. A situational dramedy about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:11.520
dysfunctional families. A mix of urban fantasy,&nbsp;
cultural myths, and science-fiction. Gargoyles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:16.297
is all of those things, but keep watching to find&nbsp;
out what only adults notice when they watch it!

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:22.200
Think back to the 1980s and early '90s, and you&nbsp;
won't recall too many shows that embrace long-form&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:27.660
storytelling the way many shows do today. Plots&nbsp;
that move their way through dozens of episodes or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:32.880
more and characters whose stories grow and evolve&nbsp;
over the course of shows from that era are fairly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:37.980
rare. Alongside other like-minded programs&nbsp;
such as X-Men: The Animated Series, Disney's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:43.440
Gargoyles helped normalize animated television&nbsp;
following characters across series-long journeys.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:48.000
Characters grow and change from episode&nbsp;
to episode; their relationships evolve,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.840
and they learn lessons that carry them&nbsp;
into future stories. At the same time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:56.400
events that take place as far back as the&nbsp;
pilot episode — set a thousand years in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:01.260
past — remain relevant well into the second&nbsp;
season. According to creator Greg Weisman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:06.180
his staff of writers worked hard to create a&nbsp;
complex web of connected stories, characters, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:12.240
events that build a rich series-spanning tapestry.&nbsp;
Weisman wrote on his website's expansive FAQ,

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:16.980
"Some of it was dumb luck. Serendipity [...]&nbsp;
[other times] we planned ahead […] Personally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:18.540
I had some long-term plans."

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:23.340
Originally, Weisman envisioned Gargoyles&nbsp;
as one sprawling, serialized saga,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:27.600
which has long been the custom for comic&nbsp;
books and daytime soap operas. In the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:29.980
that approach became key to the show's appeal.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.980
In many kids' action cartoons, heroes are&nbsp;
good, villains are bad, and that's all you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:39.900
need to know. In Gargoyles, things aren't quite&nbsp;
so black and white. The show includes baddies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:44.340
with agendas that aren't necessarily limited&nbsp;
to total destruction or world domination.

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:50.340
Xanatos — a ranked member of the Illuminati — is&nbsp;
mostly driven by wealth and power, only resorting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:55.380
to violence when it's necessary. Surprisingly,&nbsp;
he becomes an ally of Goliath and company by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.380 --> 00:02:00.240
the end of the series. Demona — a villainous&nbsp;
gargoyle — is determined to wipe out humanity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:05.400
viewing herself as a crusader for justice.&nbsp;
Derek Maza, who becomes the mutant Talon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:10.020
has a tragic backstory and mostly turns to&nbsp;
crime as a means to regain his humanity.

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:13.500
Some antagonists aren't really&nbsp;
villains at all — more victims&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:17.580
of unfortunate misunderstandings. The&nbsp;
immortal man Macbeth initially seeks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:21.840
to use the gargoyles for his own vengeful&nbsp;
ends, but eventually reveals himself as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:26.100
noble warrior whose anguish twisted&nbsp;
him into an unscrupulous antihero.

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:30.540
The kids in the audience might not realize&nbsp;
these details sets Gargoyles apart from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:34.540
the afterschool animation pack, but&nbsp;
adults can certainly pick up on it.

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:40.260
Apart from a routine fight for justice, Gargoyles&nbsp;
often centers its stories on the timeless lessons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:45.480
of parenting. The elder gargoyle Hudson acts&nbsp;
as the family's wise, grandfather-like figure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:51.060
offering Goliath counsel on how to best teach&nbsp;
his youthful clan. Goliath often fills the role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:55.560
of a reluctant surrogate father to the younger&nbsp;
gargoyles Lexington, Brooklyn, and Broadway.

00:02:56.520 --> 00:03:01.260
"The three of you may explore. But stay close&nbsp;
to the building, and stay out of sight."

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:07.380
Goliath eventually finds himself with&nbsp;
his own direct offspring — Angela,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:12.720
his daughter with Demona. Several stories focus&nbsp;
on Goliath and Angela's relationship — Goliath&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:17.700
struggles to accept her at first, but he learns&nbsp;
to embrace his new fatherly role over time.

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:23.820
Other complex parent-child relationships include&nbsp;
Xanatos and his father, and Goliath and his clone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:28.980
Thailog. Any parent who has had to watch their&nbsp;
child go astray can relate to the pain Goliath&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:33.180
feels watching Thailog turn evil. The show's&nbsp;
parental themes encompass human characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:38.076
gargoyles, and the magical beings of&nbsp;
Avalon, but they might fly over kids' heads.

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:43.680
Gargoyles began airing shortly after&nbsp;
the 1994 conclusion of sci-fi TV staple,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:47.640
Star Trek: The Next Generation, and&nbsp;
immediately became a new home for two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:52.560
of TNG's biggest stars. Jonathan Frakes&nbsp;
and Marina Sirtis — who play Commander&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:57.660
William Riker and Counselor Deanna Troi,&nbsp;
respectively — voice David Xanatos and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:02.220
Demona. But Frakes and Sirtis weren't the&nbsp;
only actors to cross over from Star Trek.

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:07.320
During the original two-season run of Gargoyles,&nbsp;
nearly a dozen actors from multiple Star Trek&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:13.500
series lent their vocal talents. Michael Dorn —&nbsp;
Worf on TNG — voices Goliath's brother Coldstone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:19.200
Brent Spiner — the iconic TNG android Data —&nbsp;
breathes life into the mischievous trickster Puck.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:25.440
Nichelle Nichols — Uhura in the original 1960s&nbsp;
Star Trek series — voices Diane, the mother of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:31.740
Talon and Elisa Maza. Star Trek: Voyager star Kate&nbsp;
Mulgrew even appears as the magical queen Titania.

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:35.100
Other notable Star Trek alumni&nbsp;
— including LeVar Burton,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:40.090
Colm Meaney, and Avery Brooks — make one-off&nbsp;
appearances that liven up their episodes.

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:45.120
We talk about diversity and representation&nbsp;
in sci-fi and fantasy much more today than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:50.400
we did in the '90s. In that respect, Gargoyles&nbsp;
was way ahead of its time by showcasing a wide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:56.040
array of cultures and backgrounds. Elisa Maza —&nbsp;
the show's sole human lead character — was not a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.040 --> 00:05:00.540
typical cartoon hero by mid-'90s standards.&nbsp;
When we meet her family, we learn that her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:04.440
father is of Native American descent, and&nbsp;
her mother's family comes from Nigeria.

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:09.180
Heroes and villains from Gargoyles hail&nbsp;
from across the globe, with BIPOC characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:13.740
appearing regularly throughout the series.&nbsp;
During the Avalon World Tour arc in Season 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:18.120
Gargoyles introduces the viewers to&nbsp;
several new figures — human and gargoyle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:22.860
alike — from countries including Japan,&nbsp;
Egypt, and Guatemala. The wide range of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:27.395
cultural and ethnic backgrounds represented on&nbsp;
Gargoyles helped it become a landmark series.

00:05:27.420 --> 00:05:31.560
Alongside its many sci-fi,&nbsp;
fantasy, and magical elements,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:36.120
Gargoyles weaves plenty of real-world&nbsp;
cultural myths into its ever-expansive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:40.500
universe. In the world of Gargoyles — which&nbsp;
is itself inspired by old Scottish tales of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:45.000
stone statues — folklore is entirely real,&nbsp;
and the source of a number of supernatural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:48.960
beings seen throughout the series. Sometimes&nbsp;
they're friends, and sometimes they're foes.

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:54.600
In the episode "The Hound of Ulster," Goliath,&nbsp;
Elisa, and Angela arrive in Ireland and encounter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:59.700
a hero and a villain from local mythology —&nbsp;
the warrior Cú Chulainn and his greatest enemy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:05.040
a malevolent banshee. The episode "Mark of the&nbsp;
Panther" sees the gang in Africa — where they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:09.780
meet Anansi, a major folktale character&nbsp;
who takes the form of a giant spider. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:13.980
the episode "Double Jeopardy," they even&nbsp;
encounter the Egyptian death god, Anubis.

00:06:13.980 --> 00:06:18.180
"Rich and poor, young and&nbsp;
old, all are equal in death."

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:23.220
To go along with these historical legends,&nbsp;
Gargoyles also includes some modern myths&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:27.180
that some kids probably assume are just&nbsp;
wild animated adventures. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:32.880
the episode "Sentinel" reveals the fictional alien&nbsp;
origins of the Easter Island heads. Apparently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:36.279
Greg Weisman's love of tall tales&nbsp;
isn't limited to ancient legends.

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:41.640
Each Gargoyles episode is more than a mere&nbsp;
adventure: they often end with an important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:46.500
message — a lesson for kids and adults alike.&nbsp;
Of course, many children's shows from the '80s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:51.180
and '90s attempt to cultivate positive values&nbsp;
in their young audiences, but Gargoyles never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:55.620
gets as heavy-handed as the likes of G.I.&nbsp;
Joe or Captain Planet and the Planeteers.

00:06:55.620 --> 00:07:00.540
Nearly every episode of Gargoyles puts a major&nbsp;
character into a difficult situation with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.540 --> 00:07:04.980
challenging moral dilemma. They might make a&nbsp;
critical mistake, but they usually learn a lesson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:09.540
in the process. Some of these lessons are as&nbsp;
simple as judging between right and wrong; other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:13.920
times, the lesson might pertain to an internal&nbsp;
problem a character needs to focus on overcoming.

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:18.900
The series tackles various social issues,&nbsp;
too. The landmark episode "Deadly Force"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:24.660
addresses gun violence when Broadway accidentally&nbsp;
shoots Elisa, causing him to despise firearms. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.660 --> 00:07:29.400
episode "A Lighthouse in the Sea of Time" brings&nbsp;
up the difficult topic of adult illiteracy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:34.080
with Hudson struggling with shame due to his&nbsp;
inability to read. After losing his way at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:38.880
daybreak, Hudson meets a new friend who teaches&nbsp;
him it's never too late to learn something new.

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:44.100
The series deals with important topics like&nbsp;
crime, poverty, and class inequality, leaving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:48.442
viewers with something to think about — even if&nbsp;
the kids watching don't realize it immediately.

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:53.460
As nonhuman beasts living in New York&nbsp;
City, Goliath and his clan are forced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:57.960
to keep their existence a secret. They hide&nbsp;
from the fear and hostility they correctly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.960 --> 00:08:02.520
expect from ordinary humans. Yet, despite&nbsp;
their cautious posture towards outsiders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:06.660
Goliath forms a bond with Elisa,&nbsp;
leading to a star-crossed romance.

00:08:06.660 --> 00:08:11.280
However, it's the story of Demona might best&nbsp;
exemplify the show's attempts to explore the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:17.400
roots of xenophobia. In recurring flashbacks&nbsp;
that begin in 994 AD, Goliath's onetime mate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:21.900
becomes immortal, and witnesses mankind's&nbsp;
brutality toward her people throughout the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:27.660
centuries. She and her numerous gargoyle clans are&nbsp;
persecuted and exterminated by the humans led by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.260 --> 00:08:32.580
Gillecomgain, a gargoyle hunter who passes his&nbsp;
irrational hatred down through the generations.

00:08:32.580 --> 00:08:34.290
"Demona!"

00:08:34.290 --> 00:08:39.540
"The Hunter! How many times must I destroy you?"

00:08:39.540 --> 00:08:43.800
Over the course of its run, Gargoyles&nbsp;
presents bigotry and intolerance as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:47.400
more dangerous than any villain. In the&nbsp;
final episodes of the second season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.400 --> 00:08:51.000
Gillecomgain's descendants stir&nbsp;
up public fear of gargoyles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:55.800
and it takes a newly reformed Xanatos to save&nbsp;
them. Ultimately, Gargoyles demonstrates the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.800 --> 00:09:00.577
dangers of intolerance, hopefully influencing&nbsp;
its young audience to be loving towards others.

00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:06.540
The drama on Gargoyles is far-reaching, deep,&nbsp;
and complex. Dare we call it ... Shakespearean?

00:09:06.540 --> 00:09:11.160
Tragic villains, family turmoil, and love&nbsp;
and loss form the basis for many episodes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:15.780
as is the case for the best of Shakespeare's&nbsp;
plays. Some character names of the Gargoyles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.780 --> 00:09:20.520
universe — such as Puck, Macbeth, Oberon,&nbsp;
and Titania — are directly lifted from major&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:25.440
Shakespearean characters. Considerable chunks&nbsp;
of Gargoyles mythology are borrowed from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:29.760
plays Macbeth and A Midsummer Night's Dream, and&nbsp;
the backstory of the Gargoyles villain Macbeth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:34.740
mirrors the play of the name name. At one point in&nbsp;
the series, we learn that many of the fantastical,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:39.060
mythology-based characters we've met are&nbsp;
called the Children of Oberon — they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:43.020
offspring of the King of the Fairies, as&nbsp;
referenced in A Midsummer Night's Dream.

00:09:43.020 --> 00:09:47.520
To children of the '90s, Gargoyles must have&nbsp;
seemed like any other fantasy cartoon with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:52.740
mighty gods, monsters, and magical beings. Adults&nbsp;
at the time — much as now — might notice how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.740 --> 00:09:57.749
Gargoyles informs its young audience about classic&nbsp;
literature without making it feel like homework.

00:09:57.749 --> 00:10:02.640
In addition to historical influences ranging&nbsp;
from Shakespeare to old-world folklore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:07.380
Gargoyles flies into the territory of&nbsp;
film noir and mystery, now and again. Matt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.380 --> 00:10:13.020
Bluestone — Elisa's partner in the NYPD — serves&nbsp;
as a clear homage to old-school detective stories.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.020 --> 00:10:18.120
Never is this more on display than his two solo&nbsp;
stories, "The Silver Falcon" and "Revelations."

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:23.280
Titled as a homage to the film noir classic&nbsp;
The Maltese Falcon, the former episode centers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:28.020
on Matt's quest to uncover the truth behind the&nbsp;
Illuminati — a secret society that has allegedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.020 --> 00:10:32.820
existed for centuries. Matt goes missing,&nbsp;
prompting Elisa and Broadway to team up and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.820 --> 00:10:37.620
track him down in the style of the old detective&nbsp;
movies Broadway has recently been enjoying on TV.

00:10:37.620 --> 00:10:42.240
"Revelations" serves as a follow-up to "The&nbsp;
Silver Falcon," in which an aging gangster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:47.520
called Mace Malone offers Matt membership&nbsp;
into the Illuminati. Told in flashbacks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:51.060
"Revelations" has all the hallmarks&nbsp;
of an old-school pulp mystery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.060 --> 00:10:56.301
and Gargoyles makes no attempt to hide its use&nbsp;
of tried-and-true classic film noir tropes.

00:10:56.301 --> 00:11:00.060
When you think of Gargoyles, its fantasy&nbsp;
elements are probably the first things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.060 --> 00:11:03.660
that pop into your mind. Whether it's&nbsp;
the magic spell that put the gargoyles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.660 --> 00:11:07.620
into a thousand-year slumber, or their&nbsp;
encounters with King Arthur, Macbeth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.620 --> 00:11:12.240
and the fay trickster Puck, the series&nbsp;
seems firmly planted into the fantasy genre.

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:17.400
However, Gargoyles also mixes in sci-fi&nbsp;
— including aliens, virtual realities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.400 --> 00:11:22.680
and time travel. Even in the first handful of&nbsp;
episodes, Xanatos builds an army of robotic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.680 --> 00:11:27.660
gargoyle copies. But nowhere is this blending&nbsp;
of genres more evident than in the character of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.660 --> 00:11:32.760
Coldstone, Goliath's long-lost brother. Found&nbsp;
in pieces after being smashed centuries ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.760 --> 00:11:37.680
Coldstone is resurrected by Xanatos&nbsp;
using science and sorcery — specifically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:42.660
a magic spell to reform his mind, and robotic&nbsp;
augmentations to reconstruct his body.

00:11:42.660 --> 00:11:45.600
"As you said, it's alive."

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:48.840
Though such genre-bending might&nbsp;
be more commonplace today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.840 --> 00:11:52.890
it was likely not something many&nbsp;
kids paid attention to in the 1990s.

00:11:52.890 --> 00:11:57.960
After the second season of Gargoyles, the series&nbsp;
returned for a third — featuring a new status quo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.960 --> 00:12:02.820
and an all-new title. The previous season had&nbsp;
ended with the existence of the gargoyles being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.820 --> 00:12:07.680
revealed to the world, and an unexpected&nbsp;
rescue from their persistent foe, Xanatos.

00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:13.740
Gargoyles: The Goliath Chronicles began airing&nbsp;
on Saturday mornings in 1996. It follows Goliath&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.740 --> 00:12:18.960
and his clan, living back in their original&nbsp;
castle, owned by Xanatos. In these 13 episodes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.960 --> 00:12:24.060
the gargoyle crew is hounded by a villainous&nbsp;
group of gargoyle hunters called the Quarrymen.

00:12:24.060 --> 00:12:28.800
The Quarrymen became the overarching baddies for&nbsp;
the remaining episodes, though were unable to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.800 --> 00:12:33.720
match the quality of the villains from the first&nbsp;
two seasons. The animation also looks cheaper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.720 --> 00:12:39.060
and the stories aren't quite as compelling. Many&nbsp;
Gargoyles fans agree that the revamped series was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.060 --> 00:12:43.620
a huge disappointment, with its lower quality&nbsp;
partially responsible for its cancellation.

00:12:43.620 --> 00:12:47.460
As cartoons come and go, children&nbsp;
watching in 1996 might not have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.460 --> 00:12:51.240
realized what was happening. Only adults&nbsp;
would have noticed that series creator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.240 --> 00:12:55.500
Greg Weisman was not listed as producer,&nbsp;
writer, and creator for this final season.

00:12:55.500 --> 00:13:00.540
According to Weisman, Disney didn't originally&nbsp;
plan for a third season, but their acquisition of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.540 --> 00:13:06.300
ABC led to a revival for Gargoyles as a Saturday&nbsp;
morning cartoon, rather than a show formatted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.300 --> 00:13:11.280
for the afternoon. With an entirely new creative&nbsp;
team on board, the company wanted him to take a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.280 --> 00:13:16.200
demotion from producer to editor, which ultimately&nbsp;
led to his departure. As Weisman recalled,

00:13:16.200 --> 00:13:20.000
"I tried to guide them, but they&nbsp;
didn't really listen too much."

